  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe remnants of Hurricane Isaias will move north into the Ohio \nValley Saturday night through Sunday, resulting in periods of \nmoderate to heavy rain across the region, especially late \nSaturday night into Sunday morning. Additional periods of \nmoderate to heavy rain are possible Sunday morning and \nafternoon, mainly along and east of the I-71 corridor as the \nremnants of Isaias gradually move east towards the Mid-Atlantic \nCoast. Rainfall amounts have increased slightly in this forecast\npackage, especially along and east of the I-71 corridor where \ntotals may range between 1 to 2 inches. The latest REFS \nguidance, albeit somewhat aggressive, also indicates a low \nprobability (20 to 30%) for a narrow swath of 3 inches of rain, \nthough this is likely the \"worst case\" scenario. The overall \nthreat for flooding is still considered to be low due to recent \ndry conditions and low stream/river levels. \n\nIn addition to the widespread rain, a potent southeast low-level jet \nwill bring breezy conditions into the region, with wind gusts \nprimarily in the 25 to 35 mph range, though can't rule out isolated \nhigher gusts up to 40 mph, especially in the favored downsloping \nareas.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe next chance for precipitation will be on Tuesday as a cold \nfront moves southeast through the Great Lakes. A narrow corridor\nof weak instability may form ahead of the front as surface dew \npoints increase into the low 60s which could support a few \nthunderstorms. Can't rule out a few stronger storms given the \nample mid-level shear in place, approaching 40 to 50 knots at \ntimes Tuesday afternoon.",
